        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.oe.16" n="16" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">51</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">-nia <hi rend="italic">Brti</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">-nia Brti</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.oe.17" n="17" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">51</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">so ohne</hi> nat. <hi rend="italic">Or nur;</hi> nat. <hi rend="italic">übrige</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">thus, without nat., Or only; nat. the others</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="textual" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.oe.18" n="18" source="#liebermann"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">1</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">51</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">sustinet <hi rend="italic">Brti</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">sustinet Brti</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.de.1" n="1" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">52</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">1</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Über den Plural <hi rend="italic">o.</hi> El 49, 9⁵. — Diesen Satz benutzt II Cn 57</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">On the plural see <hi rend="italic">above</hi> El 49, 9⁵. — This sentence is used by II Cn 57</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.de.2" n="2" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">52</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">‘Geburtsklasse’ Q; ähnlichen Sinnes <hi rend="italic">s. Wb: natale-lis, -licium, nativitas</hi></seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">‘class by birth’ Q; with a similar sense <hi rend="italic">see Wb: natale-lis, -licium, nativitas</hi></seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.de.3" n="3" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">52</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">villanus</hi> (<hi rend="italic">s. Wb</hi>) ist für Q ein zwar abhängiger, aber nicht unfreier Bauer. <hi rend="italic">Vgl. Gl</hi> gemeinfrei 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude"><hi rend="italic">villanus</hi> (<hi rend="italic">see Wb</hi>) is for Q a dependent, though not unfree, peasant. <hi rend="italic">Cf. Gl</hi> gemeinfrei 1</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.de.4" n="4" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">52</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de"><hi rend="italic">eorl</hi> (<hi rend="italic">s. Wb</hi>) missversteht Q im Sinne, den das Wort erst im 10. Jh. Agsächsisch gewann</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Q misunderstands <hi rend="italic">eorl</hi> (<hi rend="italic">see Wb</hi>) in the sense that the word acquired in Anglo-Saxon only in the 10th cent.</seg></note>
        <note type="liebermannNote" subtype="erklaerung" xml:id="Af_4-2.lnote.de.5" n="5" source="#liebermann-erkl"><bibl><biblScope unit="volume">3</biblScope><biblScope unit="page">52</biblScope><biblScope unit="column">2</biblScope></bibl><seg type="original" xml:lang="de">Nicht ‘dadurch’. Der verletzte Private also kann Begnadigung (<hi rend="italic">s. d. Gl</hi> 3) üben, ist aber hier Vassallitäts-[und daher wohl oft Gerichts]-herr des Verklagten. — Der Satz schliesst wie 4. 4, 1</seg><seg type="translation" xml:lang="en" source="#claude">Not ‘thereby’. The injured private person, then, can grant pardon (<hi rend="italic">see</hi> that word in <hi rend="italic">Gl</hi> 3), but here he is the lord by vassalage [and therefore probably often the judicial lord] of the accused. — The sentence ends like 4. 4, 1</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_4-2.attnote.fn.1" n="1" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">66</biblScope></bibl><label>ymb</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">ymbe</hi>. H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_4-2.attnote.fn.2" n="2" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">66</biblScope></bibl><label>sierwe</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">syrwie</hi>. B &amp; H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="footnote" xml:id="Af_4-2.attnote.fn.3" n="3" source="#attenborough"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">66</biblScope></bibl><label>getriowe</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en"><hi rend="italic">getreowie</hi>. B. <hi rend="italic">getrewsie</hi>. H.</seg></note>
        <note type="editorNote" subtype="translationFootnote" xml:id="Af_4-2.whinote.n.2" n="2" source="#whitelock"><bibl><biblScope unit="page">411</biblScope></bibl><label>noble</label><seg type="original" xml:lang="en">An old rhyming formula, <hi rend="italic">ge ceorle ge eorle,</hi> is used, in which <hi rend="italic">eorl</hi> retains its otherwise obsolete sense of nobleman.</seg></note>
      </div>
